Vazirmatn — Arabic-script subset, embedded in the built page
============================================================

vazirmatn-arabic-subset.woff2 is not an upstream file. It is produced from a
pinned upstream release by tools/subset-font.sh, which verifies the archive
digest before touching it, so this binary is reproducible rather than trusted.

  upstream         https://github.com/rastikerdar/vazirmatn
  release          v33.003  (npm vazirmatn@33.0.3)
  source font      misc/Non-Latin/fonts/variable/Vazirmatn-NL[wght].ttf
  archive sha512   fbjNc0CMjazZpIegWzz9OHGzI1APFboT+x7ZecXlUCtDe/nkyx7gtCTZnWS/+eeXG7fbfXymCPKJI8EsnM3iqw==
  licence          SIL Open Font License 1.1 — OFL.txt in this directory

  wght axis        pinned to 400:700
  unicodes         U+0600-06FF, U+200C-200F, U+2066-2069, U+FB50-FDFF,
                   U+FE70-FEFF, U+00A0, U+00AB, U+00BB, U+2013, U+2014, U+2026
  features         calt ccmp fina init liga locl medi rlig ss01 tnum
                   kern mark mkmk

  result           530 glyphs, 363 cmap entries
  woff2 bytes      31944
  base64 bytes     42592
  sha256           349d2f32c68726ced46a0344214a58d98bfa1d49e633a2fd2369daed01e745c7

Rebuild and confirm the digest is unchanged:

  bash tools/subset-font.sh
  sha256sum src/fonts/vazirmatn-arabic-subset.woff2

The Non-Latin build is deliberate: the system UI stack already renders Latin,
Cyrillic and CJK better than a subset would, and a Latin-bearing build costs
90 KB of base64 instead of 42 KB. The @font-face declaration carries the same
unicode-range, so the face is never applied to Latin text even when the
interface language is Persian or Arabic.


Twemoji Country Flags — the flag badge, embedded in the built page
==================================================================

twemoji-country-flags.woff2 is not an upstream file either. It is produced by
tools/subset-flag-font.sh from a pinned release of the font TalkJS publishes as
"Twemoji Country Flags" (itself a subset of Mozilla's Twemoji build). The script
verifies the archive digest before touching it.

  upstream         https://github.com/talkjs/country-flag-emoji-polyfill
  release          npm country-flag-emoji-polyfill@0.1.10
  source font      package/dist/TwemojiCountryFlags.woff2
  archive sha512   5O50p2mq/d8+4AfHz3monRQaBA4TIQ0XcKqJUhCYR6SMsLqlO4m8f87og6R1D68Z1sNXGkjLkcEccuzC6T9i5g==
  licence          artwork: Twemoji, CC-BY 4.0; build: MIT — TWEMOJI-LICENSE.txt

  unicodes         U+1F1E6-1F1FF (the regional indicator symbols only)
  units per em     64 (the source uses 512)
  built with       fonttools 4.63.0, brotli 1.2.0

  result           1641 glyphs, 258 flags
  woff2 bytes      51560
  base64 bytes     68748
  sha256           806220bbb9728915709267d5c7ca99b69212f9acf6c4007bd0bff27fe5ea5586

Rebuild and confirm the digest is unchanged:

  bash tools/subset-flag-font.sh
  sha256sum src/fonts/twemoji-country-flags.woff2

Changes from the upstream font: the three subdivision flags (tag sequences the
page never draws) are removed, hinting is removed, and the outlines are scaled
from 512 to 64 units per em. At the badge's 16 to 34 px a unit is at most half a
pixel, and the flags were compared side by side in Chromium, WebKit and Firefox
before and after. The @font-face declaration carries unicode-range U+1F1E6-1F1FF,
so the face is used for flags and nothing else, and a browser only decodes it on
a page that shows a flag.
